+The 94 appraised works support a conditional benefit from multimodal monitoring. In four-state welding classification, combining image and audio raised accuracy from 90.2\% for the stronger single input to 96.1\%. In weld-penetration classification, both individual inputs and their combination reached 100\% on the clean test set \parencite{fan_dmaw_2026,hua_anti-interference_2026}. In human--robot handover, adding gripper state to video and force/torque reduced five-class accuracy \parencite{thoduka_multimodal_2024}. Taken together, these comparisons locate the architectural question more precisely: for a given target and observation window, when does a second input retain complementary information, and when does a learned fusion rule use it more effectively than a strong single predictor or a fixed combination under the same fitting and reliability conditions?
 
